#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079726 :tutorial/content "Consider the following sequence:\n$1,\\quad 3,\\quad 5,\\quad 7,\\quad 9,\\quad \\ldots$\nNotice that the difference (or \"jump\") between two consecutive numbers is always the same.\n\n![](images/5a/5a4433d95492b9895793bc497fce887e3a39986a4f0c686ebe8a5d9b54d4f3dc.png)\n\nA sequence where the jump never changes is called an **arithmetic sequence.**\n\nWe call this jump the **common difference**, and represent it using the letter $d$. In this particular case, the common difference is $d=2$." :tutorial/id #uuid "134e25b5-1134-520f-9bd7-cc4176f2fed8" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8907 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}]}
